{"id": "836c6881-f3ec-4539-9ac5-fafe0947a02d", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "CloudTrail-related queries seem to fall under the mandatory delegation rules, so I should hand this off to a sub-agent with infrastructure investigation skills rather than calling CloudTrail APIs directly.

This is really a configuration check rather than root-cause work, so I'll share my plan with the user first, then launch the sub-agent. The prompt should cover trail status, event selectors including S3 object-level and Lambda Invoke data events, advanced event selectors, logging and multi-region settings, across all relevant accounts and regions, using read-only access throughout.

"}]}

{"id": "539ec389-d755-41c9-af60-a5e296552f60", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll check your CloudTrail configuration. S3 object deletes and Lambda invocations are data events, and CloudTrail only records those if a trail is set up to capture them. I'll look at the trails, their event selectors and their logging status.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_FWOAfymNlfgrTD5IG2lYbW", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "cloudtrail-trail-coverage-check", "agent_type": "generic", "preloaded_skills": ["investigating-infrastructure-changes"], "prompt": "{
  "task": "Audit CloudTrail trail configuration to determine whether (a) S3 object deletions (e.g. DeleteObject, DeleteObjects) and (b) Lambda function invocations (Invoke) would be recorded by CloudTrail.",
  "context": "User asked: 'If someone deletes an object from one of our S3 buckets or invokes a Lambda function, would CloudTrail record it? Check our trails.' Key background: S3 object-level operations and Lambda Invoke are DATA events, which are only logged if a trail (or event data store) has data event selectors configured for them. S3 bucket-level ops (DeleteBucket etc.) and Lambda management APIs (CreateFunction, UpdateFunctionCode) are management events. This is a read-only configuration audit, do not make any changes.",
  "resources": ["All AWS accounts associated with this agent space (start with 012345678901), agent space region us-east-1 plus any other regions where trails exist; use list_associations/get_agent_space as needed to find accounts"],
  "timeframe": "Current configuration (now)",
  "criteria": "For each account: list all trails (describe_trails with includeShadowTrails, list_trails) and for each: name, home region, IsMultiRegionTrail, IsOrganizationTrail, S3 bucket destination, whether it is currently logging (get_trail_status: IsLogging, latest delivery errors), management event settings (read/write, ReadWriteType), and data event configuration from get_event_selectors (both classic EventSelectors and AdvancedEventSelectors). Determine specifically: do any trails capture S3 data events (resources.type AWS::S3::Object) covering all buckets or which specific buckets, with WriteOnly/ReadOnly/All; and do any capture Lambda data events (AWS::Lambda::Function) covering all functions or which ones. Also check CloudTrail Lake event data stores (list_event_data_stores) and their advanced event selectors if any. Note gaps: trails not logging, single-region trails, buckets/functions not covered, advanced selectors that filter out eventName DeleteObject/Invoke. Optionally list the S3 buckets and Lambda functions to state which are covered vs not. If feasible, also do a quick lookup of recent DeleteObject or Invoke events to empirically confirm, but note lookup_events does not return data events so absence there is not conclusive. Return a concise summary: a per-trail table plus a clear verdict for S3 object delete and Lambda invoke."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Audit CloudTrail trails and data event coverage for S3 and Lambda"}], "created_at": null}

